WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.960
A neuron listens with its dendrites and speaks through its axon.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.020
How does it keep the two apart?

3
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.090
Dendrites are usually several, short and branched.

4
00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.290
They receive input from other cells.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.420
The axon is usually single, even in width and often very long.

6
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.520
It carries the action potential to other cells.

7
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:29.033
Polarity is the word for a cell with two distinct domains, different in structure

8
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:30.033
and molecules.

9
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:32.423
The difference is molecular.

10
00:00:33.543 --> 00:00:38.583
Dendrites carry the protein MAP2, receptors for neurotransmitters, and often spines.

11
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:44.533
The axon carries other proteins, such as tau, and its own channels and vesicle

12
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:45.533
machinery.

13
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:50.647
A neuron must sort thousands of proteins to the right side, and keep them there for

14
00:00:50.647 --> 00:00:51.647
decades.

15
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.600
Now a puzzle from a dish.

16
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.760
Take a mature neuron, and cut its axon close to the cell body.

17
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.870
Cut another neuron's axon far from the cell body.

18
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:03.890
What grows out in each case?

19
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.900
Cut close, and one of the dendrites can grow out as a new axon.

20
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:17.493
Cut far, and the old axon simply regrows.

21
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:20.657
So axon is not a permanent label.

22
00:01:21.517 --> 00:01:25.437
It is a state the cell keeps, and losing most of the axon can reset it.

23
00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.910
That raises two questions.

24
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.030
How is the axon won?

25
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.390
And once won, how is it held?

26
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:37.483
Here is the map: a young neuron in a dish, as one neurite pulls ahead.

27
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:40.367
In the middle sits the cell body.

28
00:01:41.547 --> 00:01:44.027
Several short minor neurites grow out from it, all alike.

29
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:49.227
Each neurite ends in a growth cone, the motile tip of a growing neurite.

30
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:53.037
One has pulled ahead, and is becoming the axon.

31
00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:55.217
The others will become dendrites.

32
00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:01.770
The axon's first few tens of micrometres, the proximal axon, is where the initial

33
00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:02.770
segment will form.

34
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.830
The idea to carry through: an axon is won in a race, and then held.

35
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.860
In a useful model, positive feedback in each neurite, strong enough to compound a

36
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.060
lead, plus competition among them, decides the race.

37
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.680
Growth in simple proportion to length picks no winner.

38
00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:26.933
Then structures in the axon help hold the win: its microtubule tracks, and a gate

39
00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:27.933
near its start.

40
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:31.513
Loosen the hold, and the axon can lose features.

41
00:02:32.473 --> 00:02:34.873
Cut almost all of it away, and the race reopens.

42
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:39.567
Won, then held. But this race was seen in a dish.

43
00:02:40.487 --> 00:02:41.547
Next, we watch it begin.
